    "Ancient History": "ANCIENT HISTORY – COMPLETE NOTES\n\n1. PREHISTORIC INDIA\n\n• Prehistory refers to the period before written records.\n• Divided into Paleolithic, Mesolithic, and Neolithic phases.\n\nPaleolithic Age (Old Stone Age):\n• Time Period: 2.5 million BCE – 10,000 BCE\n• Tools: Crude stone tools (hand axes, choppers)\n• Economy: Hunting and gathering\n• Sites: Bhimbetka, Hunsgi, Kurnool caves\n\nMesolithic Age (Middle Stone Age):\n• Time Period: 10,000 BCE – 6,000 BCE\n• Tools: Microliths\n• Domestication of animals begins\n• Sites: Bagor (Rajasthan), Adamgarh\n\nNeolithic Age (New Stone Age):\n• Time Period: 7000 BCE onwards\n• Agriculture begins\n• Polished stone tools\n• Domestication of animals\n• Sites: Mehrgarh, Burzahom, Chirand\n\n\n2. INDUS VALLEY CIVILIZATION\n\n• Time Period: 2500–1900 BCE\n• Major Cities: Harappa, Mohenjo-daro, Dholavira, Lothal\n\nFeatures:\n• Urban planning with grid system\n• Drainage system well developed\n• Granaries and warehouses\n• Great Bath (Mohenjo-daro)\n\nEconomy:\n• Agriculture (wheat, barley)\n• Trade with Mesopotamia\n\nReligion:\n• Mother Goddess worship\n• Proto-Shiva (Pashupati seal)\n\nDecline:\n• Climate change, river shifts, ecological factors\n\n\n3. VEDIC AGE\n\nEarly Vedic (1500–1000 BCE):\n• Rigvedic society\n• Pastoral economy\n• Tribal polity\n\nLater Vedic (1000–600 BCE):\n• Agriculture dominant\n• Iron usage increases\n• Rise of Janapadas\n\nReligion:\n• Nature worship (Indra, Agni, Varuna)\n• Later shift towards rituals and Brahmanical dominance\n\nLiterature:\n• Vedas, Brahmanas, Aranyakas, Upanishads\n\n\n4. MAHAJANAPADAS\n\n• 16 Mahajanapadas mentioned in Buddhist texts\n• Important: Magadha, Kosala, Avanti, Vatsa\n\nMagadha Rise:\n• Bimbisara, Ajatashatru\n• Advantages: Fertile land, iron ore, river access\n\n\n5. RELIGIOUS MOVEMENTS\n\nBuddhism:\n• Founder: Gautama Buddha\n• Four Noble Truths\n• Eightfold Path\n\nJainism:\n• Founder: Mahavira\n• Triratnas (Right faith, knowledge, conduct)\n\n\n6. MAURYAN EMPIRE\n\n• Founder: Chandragupta Maurya\n• Kautilya’s Arthashastra\n\nAshoka:\n• Kalinga War\n• Dhamma policy\n• Rock and Pillar Edicts\n\nAdministration:\n• Centralized governance\n\n\n7. POST-MAURYAN PERIOD\n\n• Sungas, Kanvas, Indo-Greeks, Kushanas\n\nKushanas:\n• Kanishka\n• Gandhara art\n\nSatavahanas:\n• Deccan rule\n• Prakrit language\n\n\n
\n\n8. GUPTA EMPIRE\n\n• Period: 4th–6th century CE\n• Founder: Chandragupta I\n\nImportant Rulers:\n• Samudragupta – “Napoleon of India”\n• Chandragupta II (Vikramaditya)\n\nFeatures:\n• Golden Age of India\n• Advancement in science, literature, art\n\nScience:\n• Aryabhata – Astronomy, zero\n• Varahamihira – Astronomy\n\nLiterature:\n• Kalidasa – Abhijnanasakuntalam\n\nArt:\n• Ajanta paintings\n• Temple architecture begins\n\n\n9. SCIENCE & TECHNOLOGY\n\n• Sushruta – Surgery\n• Charaka – Medicine\n• Aryabhata – Mathematics and astronomy\n\nKey Concepts:\n• Zero and decimal system\n• Trigonometry\n\n\n10. ART & ARCHITECTURE\n\n• Stupas (Buddhist)\n• Rock-cut caves (Ajanta, Ellora)\n• Early temples (Nagara and Dravida styles)\n\n\n11. SANGAM AGE\n\n• Tamil literature\n• Three Sangams\n\nFeatures:\n• Trade and urbanization\n• Roman trade\n\n\n12. IMPORTANT TERMS & FACTS\n\n• Vedangas – six auxiliary disciplines\n• Nirukta – etymology\n• Upanishads – philosophical texts\n\n\n13. KEY THEMES FOR PRELIMS\n\n• Urbanization (Harappan)\n• Iron usage (Later Vedic)\n• State formation (Mahajanapadas)\n• Religion (Buddhism, Jainism)\n• Empire building (Maurya, Gupta)\n\n"
\n\n--- END OF ANCIENT HISTORY NOTES ---"
